<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enewetak</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Eniwetok</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enewetak is a large coral atoll in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a>. American forces captured it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in February 1944, and from 1948 to 1958 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> carried out 43 nuclear tests there, including Ivy Mike in 1952, the first full-scale hydrogen bomb. Its people were moved away and returned only after a partial clean-up in 1980; contaminated soil was sealed under the concrete Runit Dome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enewetak.jpg" alt="Enewetak" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enewetak_Atoll_-_2014-02-10_-_Landsat_8_-_15m.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Enewetak is a coral atoll of 40 islands in the Pacific, forming a legislative district of the Ralik Chain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a>. Its land area is under 5.85 square kilometres and no point is higher than 5 metres, and the islands surround a deep lagoon 80 kilometres in circumference. The atoll sits on a seamount of basalt formed in the late Cretaceous. Austronesian islanders settled the atoll, which has been inhabited since about 1,000 BCE. The Spanish explorer Álvaro de Saavedra Cerón reached it on 10 October 1529 and named it Los Jardines. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government spelled it Eniwetok until 1974.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a> in 1914, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> mandated them to Japan in 1920. In November 1942 the Japanese built an airfield on Engebi Island, and in January 1944 a brigade arrived to defend the atoll but could not finish its fortifications before the American attack. In the Battle of Eniwetok in February 1944 American forces took the atoll in a five-day amphibious operation. It then became a major naval advance base, with an average of 488 ships present daily in the first half of July 1944.</p><p>After the war the atoll came under American control as part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a> became independent in 1986. The Americans evacuated the residents many times, often against their will, and used the atoll as part of the Pacific Proving Grounds. Forty-three nuclear tests were fired there from 1948 to 1958. Ivy Mike, the first hydrogen bomb test, took place in late 1952, vaporised the islet of Elugelab and created two new elements, fermium and einsteinium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A cleanup began in 1977. Over three years the American military mixed more than 80,000 cubic metres of contaminated soil and debris with cement and sealed it in the crater of the nuclear test of 5 May 1958 on Runit Island, under a dome of 358 concrete panels. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> declared the southern and western islands safe in 1980, and residents returned that year. In 2017 the Australian Broadcasting Corporation reported that rising seas were seeping into the dome. The Marshall Islands Nuclear Claims Tribunal made an award to the people of Enewetak in 2000, including 107.8 million US dollars for environmental restoration. The atoll had 296 residents in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enewetak Atoll” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1946 to 1958 the United States conducted 67 nuclear tests at Bikini and <b>Enewetak</b>, including the Castle Bravo explosion of 1954, which contaminated Rongelap.</blockquote><a href="/state/trust-territory-of-the-pacific-islands">Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands</a></li></ul></section>
